============================================================
3. INPUT DATA
============================================================

There are 22 ZIP datasets.

ScanData001 has already been completed.

Process only the remaining 21 datasets.

The ZIP files may have different internal folder structures.

Therefore, for every ZIP:

1. Identify the ZIP.
2. Extract it into a temporary working directory.
3. Inspect its directory structure.
4. Identify the actual structural MRI/MPRAGE data.
5. Determine whether it is DICOM or NIfTI.
6. Determine the correct MRI session/sequence.
7. Process only the correct structural MRI.

Do NOT assume that every ZIP has exactly the same internal structure.

Do NOT process unrelated MRI sequences such as functional MRI, resting-state fMRI, diffusion MRI, field maps, or other scans as structural MRI.

============================================================
4. TEMPORARY EXTRACTION
============================================================

Use a temporary extraction area such as:

C:\Users\User\Documents\neurofusion\temp_folder

For example:

temp_folder\
    ScanData002\
    ScanData003\
    ...

Only one dataset needs to be actively extracted at a time if possible.

This is important because disk space should be conserved.

After a dataset has been completely processed and verified, its temporary extracted raw folder may be deleted.

However:

NEVER delete processed_data.

NEVER delete the final feature CSVs.

NEVER delete the MNI registered images before verification.

NEVER delete transforms or QC outputs before verification.

============================================================
5. SUBJECT IDENTIFICATION
============================================================

For every dataset:

- Detect all valid structural MRI subjects.
- Determine the subject ID from the existing dataset naming convention.
- Preserve the original subject ID wherever possible.
- Do not invent random subject IDs.
- Do not silently rename subjects.

If a subject ID already exists in the processed feature matrix, check whether it is genuinely the same subject/data.

Do not create duplicate rows for the same subject.

If the same subject appears more than once because of multiple MRI sessions, inspect the sessions and use the same selection logic already established for ScanData001.

If the correct session cannot be determined automatically, STOP and report the ambiguity rather than choosing randomly.

